    "body": "Artificial intelligence is playing a bigger role in business decisions, but most leaders still want a person to make the final call. In a new Körber repo rt , 73% of executives surveyed said they were uncomfortable letting technology have the final say.\nWhat’s Related\nAmong U.S. respondents, 77% said AI was more involved in decisions, and 78% said decision-making had become more complex. Across the full survey, nearly three-quarters said too much data could make decisions harder or that decisions could stall while teams waited for information.\nKörber released its AI and Decision Complexity Report on Nov. 18. Censuswide surveyed 2,000 senior leaders, split evenly between the U.S. and Germany, at organizations with more than $250 million in annual revenue and at least 250 employees.\nSupply chain leads on AI use\nSupply chain and distribution leaders reported the highest AI involvement in decisions, at 92%. Handling exceptions and dealing with variability were challenges for 36% of respondents, while 24% cited trouble getting systems to work together.\nHealthcare and life sciences leaders reported slower AI adoption, at 60%. Their most frequently cited challenges were connecting systems (48%) and workforce skills (46%). Körber based each industry breakdown on responses from 50 U.S. executives.\nRELATED: 10 Routine Supply Chain Tasks You Should Use AI For\nAI spending plans are similar\nAbout 59% of leaders at organizations making companywide changes planned to invest heavily in AI tools that help people make decisions. The share was nearly identical among organizations still in the pilot phase.\nLeaders in the companywide group were more likely to say their organizations were performing well, at 64% compared with 42% in the pilot group.\n“Most organizations are planning or executing significant AI-related investments, but spending alone won’t secure a return on investment ,” said Helena Garriga, a member of Körber’s executive board and president of its supply chain business.\nFour things that help AI pay off\nKörber points to four things companies can put in place to get more out of their AI spending.\nTimely, reliable data that reaches teams when they need to act.\nClear responsibility for who makes the final call and when.\nA process for handling exceptions that need human judgment.\nHuman oversight based on the decision, with clear rules for what can run automatically and what needs approval.",
